    title: "What the Three Steps Mean",
    tags: ["meaning", "symbolism", "three steps meaning", "interpretation", "what does it mean", "significance", "deeper meaning", "philosophy", "three steps"],
    body: "The three steps of Vamanan are among the most layered symbols in Hindu mythology. They operate on multiple levels simultaneously:\n\nOn the cosmic level: the first stride covers the earth (Bhumi), the second covers the heavens (Dyauh), and the third has nowhere to go but the self — Bali's own head. The three steps encompass all of measurable reality and then step beyond it.\n\nOn the philosophical level: the first step represents all that you can see and own — the material world. The second represents all that you can imagine and desire — the world of aspiration and dream. When both are given away, the only thing left is the self. Bali's offering of his head is the surrender of ego — and in that surrender, the self is not destroyed but preserved eternally.\n\nOn the ethical level: the story teaches that true generosity has no limit and no self-protection. Bali knew he would lose everything, yet he kept his word. The three steps are a test of whether your dharma (duty) can survive your own loss.\n\nVamanan's message: the infinite arrives only after the finite has been fully surrendered.",
