    body: "Onam is the state festival of Kerala, celebrated over ten days (Atham to Thiruvonam) during the Malayalam month of Chingam (August–September). At its heart is the promise of Vamanan: because Bali gave himself willingly and kept his word even unto loss, Vamanan granted him a boon — once a year, King Mahabali may return to his beloved people to see them happy and prosperous.\n\nOnam is that return. Every element of the celebration is a welcome:\n\n• Pookkalam: intricate flower carpets (also called Athappookkalam) laid daily before homes, growing in size each day, welcoming the king with beauty.\n\n• Sadya: a grand vegetarian feast served on banana leaves, featuring 20–30 dishes including avial, sambar, payasam, and parippu curry — a meal of abundance fit for a king.\n\n• Vallamkali: snake boat races on the backwaters, especially at Aranmula and Punnamada, with long chundan boats rowed by dozens of men.\n\n• Kaikottikali and Thumbi Thullal: traditional group dances performed by women in Mundu and Kasavu sarees around the pookkalam.\n\n• Pulikali: performers painted as tigers and leopards dance through the streets of Thrissur.\n\n• Onathappan: clay or wooden figures of Mahabali and Vamanan placed beside the pookkalam.\n\nVamanan is the silent presence behind every Onam — the one who made the return possible.",
