    body: "Mahabali — known affectionately as Maveli in Kerala — was an asura (demon) king, but unlike the common image of asuras, he was just, generous, and beloved by all his subjects. Under his rule there was no lie, no poverty, no fear. His kingdom was described as a golden age where dharma stood on four legs.\n\nHe was the grandson of Prahlada, the great devotee of Vishnu, and the son of Virochana. Despite being born into the asura lineage, Bali's virtue was so great that it threatened the gods — not because he was cruel, but because he was loved. His fame spread across the three worlds, and his power grew until even Indra's throne seemed insecure.\n\nVamanan's test was not a punishment for evil — it was a reckoning with a goodness that had grown too proud of itself. Bali's grace is that he kept his word even when he knew he would lose everything. When Vamanan asked for three paces of land, Bali's guru Shukracharya warned him, but the king refused to withdraw his promise. In losing his kingdom, Bali became eternal — granted the boon of returning once a year to his people, which is celebrated as Onam.\n\nIn Kerala, Mahabali is not a villain. He is the beloved king, the generous father, the one who gave everything and was honored for it. The song 'Maveli Naadu Vaneedum Kalam' describes his reign as a time when all people were equal and none suffered.",
