    body: "The moment Bali granted the boon, Vamanan revealed his true nature: Trivikrama, the universe-spanning form. With one step he covered the earth; with the second he measured the heavens. The oceans trembled, the stars scattered, Brahma washed his feet with water from his kamandalu (which became the river Ganga), and every being saw that the small sage at the gate was the sustainer of all worlds.\n\nTrivikrama means 'the one who took three steps' — tri (three) + vikrama (stride). In Vedic tradition, this connects to the three steps of Vishnu mentioned in the Rigveda, where Vishnu's three strides encompass the earth, the atmosphere, and the heavens. The Vamanan avatar is the mythological embodiment of this ancient Vedic concept.\n\nTrivikrama is the moment the infinite shows itself through the finite — a reminder that scale is not a measure of significance, and that the smallest vessel can hold the whole cosmos. In temple iconography, Trivikrama is depicted with one leg lifted high (covering the heavens) and the other planted on the earth, with Bali kneeling beneath, offering his head.",
