    body: "Shukracharya, guru of the asuras and one of the wisest sages in the tradition, saw through Vamanan's disguise instantly. He recognized the small dwarf as Vishnu himself, come to take everything Bali had. He pulled Bali aside and warned him: 'This is no ordinary brahmachari — this is the Preserver in disguise. Withdraw your promise, or you will lose all.'\n\nBut Bali refused. His reasoning was profound: 'A king who has given his word cannot take it back — not even to save his kingdom. My promise is my dharma.' When Shukracharya persisted, Bali declared that he would rather lose his kingdom than his honor.\n\nShukracharya, in his frustration, is said to have shrunk himself to enter the spout of the water vessel to block the flow of water — but Vamanan, seeing this, pushed a blade of darbha grass into the spout, blinding one of Shukracharya's eyes. (This is why Shukracharya is sometimes depicted with one eye.)\n\nShukracharya's warning is the voice of self-preservation; Bali's answer is the voice of dharma. The story honors both: the guru for seeing the truth, and the king for choosing it.",
