    body: "Vamanan's story is not locked in scripture — it is a mirror held up to every generation. Here is why it still matters:\n\n1. The danger of unchecked pride: Bali was not evil — he was good, and his goodness became his blind spot. Power that is generous but cannot see its own limit is the most dangerous kind. Every era has its Mahabali.\n\n2. The power of keeping your word: In a world of broken promises and strategic dishonesty, Bali's refusal to withdraw his word — even knowing it would cost him everything — is a radical ethical statement. Your word is your dharma.\n\n3. True generosity has no self-protection: Bali gave knowing he would lose. That is the highest form of generosity — not giving from surplus, but giving from the knowledge that giving will cost you.\n\n4. The small can hold the infinite: Vamanan appeared as the least. In a world obsessed with scale, followers, and visibility, the story says: the small step, taken in truth, can cover the world. Do not judge by appearance.\n\n5. Loss can be transformation, not defeat: Bali did not lose — he was preserved. Sometimes what looks like losing everything is the moment you become eternal.\n\nThe teaching is simple and hard: keep your word, honor what you give, and understand that true greatness is measured not by how much you hold but by what you are willing to offer.",
