    body: "The Onam Sadya is a grand vegetarian feast served on a banana leaf, the crown jewel of Onam celebrations. It typically features 20–30 dishes, and the arrangement on the leaf follows a precise tradition. Here are the essential components:\n\n• Parippu Curry: Moong dal with ghee, the first item served — the simplest and most sacred.\n\n• Sambar: Lentil stew with vegetables and tamarind, poured over rice.\n\n• Avial: A medley of vegetables (drumstick, yam, plantain, beans) cooked with coconut and curd.\n\n• Thoran: Vegetables stir-fried with grated coconut and turmeric — cabbage thoran, bean thoran, or carrot thoran.\n\n• Olan: White pumpkin and cowpeas cooked in coconut milk.\n\n• Kootu Curry: Yam and raw banana cooked with chickpeas and coconut.\n\n• Erissery: Pumpkin or yam cooked with coconut and spices.\n\n• Pachadi: Vegetables in a yogurt-coconut base, sometimes with a sweet note.\n\n• Kichadi: Vegetables in a spiced yogurt sauce (not to be confused with North Indian khichdi).\n\n• Pickles (Achar): Lime, mango, and lemon pickles for the sharp note.\n\n• Payasam: The grand finale — sweet pudding. Parippu payasam (moong dal), Ada payasam (rice flakes), or Pazham payasam (banana).\n\n• Banana chips, pappadam, and fresh banana complete the meal.\n\nThe sadya is eaten with the hands, seated on the floor, and served from the narrow end of the banana leaf. It is a meal of abundance — a king's welcome for a king who is returning home.",
