    body: "A Pookkalam (also called Athappookkalam) is a circular floral arrangement made on the ground to welcome King Mahabali during Onam. Here is how it is traditionally made:\n\n1. The Base: Clean the ground in front of the house. Draw a concentric circle pattern — traditionally ten rings for the ten days of Onam (Atham to Thiruvonam).\n\n2. The Flowers: Use fresh, brightly colored flowers — marigold (jamanthi), chrysanthemum, thumba (small white flowers), chembarathi (hibiscus), and mandaram. The flowers are plucked and arranged by hand, petal by petal.\n\n3. The Layers: Start from the outer ring and work inward. Each ring uses a different color. The outermost ring is usually orange or yellow marigold, then alternating white thumba, red chembarathi, and yellow chrysanthemum, with a central dot of bright color.\n\n4. The Daily Growth: On Atham (day 1), the pookkalam is small — just one ring. Each day a new ring is added. By Thiruvonam (day 10), it is a large, intricate mandala of ten concentric rings.\n\n5. The Onathappan: A small clay figure of Mahabali (and sometimes Vamanan) is placed in the center.\n\nThe pookkalam is more than decoration — it is a daily meditation, an offering of beauty to the returning king, and a communal activity that brings families together each morning of Onam.",
